# Sensitive Data Logging

Never log passwords, auth tokens, session cookies, credit card numbers, CVVs, full PANs, government IDs, or other secrets. Logs are often retained, forwarded, and broadly accessible.

## Do / Don't

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — secrets end up in log aggregators
logger.info("Login attempt", { email, password });
console.log("Token issued", accessToken);
logger.debug("Payment", { cardNumber, cvv });

// ✅ GOOD — log non-sensitive identifiers only; redact the rest
logger.info("Login attempt", { email, password: "[REDACTED]" });
logger.info("Token issued", { userId, tokenType: "access" });
logger.debug("Payment", { last4: card.last4, brand: card.brand });
```

## Checklist

- Do not pass raw request bodies, headers (`Authorization`, `Cookie`), or env secrets into log statements.
- Prefer allowlisted fields over dumping whole objects (`req`, `user`, `payload`).
- If a library may auto-log requests/responses, configure redaction for sensitive keys (`password`, `token`, `authorization`, `cardNumber`, etc.).
- In tests and error messages, assert on behavior — never print fixtures that contain real credentials or production tokens.
